\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Sira (also Shira or Eshira, ISO 639-3 swj, Guthrie B.41) is a Bantu language of Gabon with about 39,000 speakers (a 2000 figure). Very little was found: no dictionary, textbook, or school material appeared in the search results. Only reference pages and linguistic articles were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes were found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Shira language. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shira_language}
\item Blanchon, J. A. (1988), article on tone in Eshira (Glottolog bibliographic record). \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/25883}
\item Blanchon, J. A. and L. Mouguiama (1997), on verb tonality in Eshira de Manji (Glottolog record). \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/97160}
\item Mouguiama, L. (1994), Glottolog record. \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/147050}
\item ASJP wordlist for B41 Shira. \url{https://asjp.clld.org/languages/B41_SHIRA}
\item ASJP wordlist for Sira. \url{https://asjp.clld.org/languages/SIRA}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None were found in the search results.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video or corpus resources were found. The ASJP wordlists are the only lexical data seen. The Royal Museum for Central Africa pottery vocabulary database lists a single Sira term: \url{https://www.africamuseum.be/en/research/discover/human_sciences/culture_society/cb-pottery/comparative/3599/vocabulary}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Sira is not known to be taught in schools. Documentation is thin, limited to a few linguistic articles on tone and short wordlists. A curriculum would first need basic documentation such as a dictionary, orthography and grammar. French is the likely language of schooling in Gabon, but the searches did not address this.

\end{document}
